          "content": {
            "size": 25976,
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"numberOfRecords\":29,\"briefRecords\":[{\"oclcNumber\":\"1222774443\",\"isbns\":[\"9780191917653\",\"0191917656\"],\"isbn13\":\"9780191917653\",\"title\":\"The agricultural revolution in prehistory : why did foragers become farmers?\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"The agricultural revolution in prehistory : why did foragers become farmers?\"},\"creator\":\"Graeme Barker\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Graeme\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"relatorCodes\":[\"aut\"],\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Barker\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2020\",\"publicationLanguage\":\"eng\",\"catalogingLanguage\":\"eng\",\"generalFormat\":\"Book\",\"specificFormat\":\"Digital\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":13,\"publisher\":\"Oxford University Press\",\"publicationPlace\":\"Oxford\",\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"Agriculture, Prehistoric\",\"Agriculture Origin\",\"Plants, Cultivated Origin\",\"Plant remains (Archaeology)\",\"Agriculture préhistorique\",\"Agriculture Origines\",\"Restes de plantes (Archéologie)\"],\"publication\":null,\"summaries\":[\"Addressing one of the most debated revolutions in the history of our species, the change from hunting and gathering to farming, this title takes a global view, and integrates an array of information from archaeology and many other disciplines, including anthropology, botany, climatology, genetics, linguistics, and zoology\"],\"summary\":\"Addressing one of the most debated revolutions in the history of our species, the change from hunting and gathering to farming, this title takes a global view, and integrates an array of information from archaeology and many other disciplines, including anthropology, botany, climatology, genetics, linguistics, and zoology\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":[{\"generalFormat\":\"Book\",\"oclcNumber\":\"64554643\",\"specificFormat\":\"PrintBook\"}],\"peerReviewed\":false,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"4669062132\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Prehistoric Transitions to Agriculture: New Overviews - The Neolithic Revolution in the Near East: Transforming the Human Landscape, by Alan H. Simmons, 2007. Tucson (AZ): University of Arizona Press; ISBN-13: 978-0-8165-2442-6 hardback £34.99 & US$55; xviii+339 pp., 31 figs., 6 tables - The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: Why Did Foragers Become Farmers, by Graeme Barker, 2006; Oxford: Oxford University Press. ISBN-13: 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £88 & US$180; xvi+598 pp., 138 figs., 15 tables - The Emergence of Agriculture: a Global View, edited by Tim Denham & Peter White, 2007. London: Routledge; ISBN-13: 978-0-415-40445-2 paperback £21.99 & US$39.95; xii+276 pp., 30 figs., 11 tables\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Prehistoric Transitions to Agriculture: New Overviews - The Neolithic Revolution in the Near East: Transforming the Human Landscape, by Alan H. Simmons, 2007. Tucson (AZ): University of Arizona Press; ISBN-13: 978-0-8165-2442-6 hardback £34.99 & US$55; xviii+339 pp., 31 figs., 6 tables - The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: Why Did Foragers Become Farmers, by Graeme Barker, 2006; Oxford: Oxford University Press. ISBN-13: 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £88 & US$180; xvi+598 pp., 138 figs., 15 tables - The Emergence of Agriculture: a Global View, edited by Tim Denham & Peter White, 2007. London: Routledge; ISBN-13: 978-0-415-40445-2 paperback £21.99 & US$39.95; xii+276 pp., 30 figs., 11 tables\"},\"creator\":\"Peter Bogucki\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Peter\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Bogucki\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2009\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[],\"publication\":\"Cambridge Archaeological Journal, 19, 200902, 111\",\"summaries\":[],\"summary\":\"\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"5974675210\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"General - Graeme Barker. The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: Why did foragers become farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-9281091 hardback £80. - Roy Ellen (ed.). Ethnobiology and the Science of Humankind (Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute Special Issue 1). viii+202 pages, 19 illu-strations, 8 tables. 2006. Oxford, Malden (MA) & Victoria, Australia: Blackwell; 978-1-4051-4589-7 paperback £19.99 & $34.95 & AUS$65.95. - Frederic Seller, Russell Greaves & Pei-Lin YU (ed.). Archaeology and Ethnoarchaeology ofMobility. xviii+290 pages, 54 illustrations, 28 tables. 2006. Gainesville (FL): University Press of Florida; 0-81302956-2 hardback $65. - Isabelle Sidera with Emmanuelle Vila & Phillippe Erikson (ed.). La chasse: pratiques sociales et symboliques (Colloques de la Maison René-Ginouvès). xiv+266 pages, 87 illustrations. 2006. Paris: De Boccard; 2-7018-0192-3 paperback. - Michael Bright. Beasts of the Field: The Revealing NaturalHistoryofAnimalsinthe Bible. xviii+346 pages. 2006. London: Robson Books; 978-1-86105-831-7 paperback £12.99 - Azar Gat. War in Human Civilization. xvi+822 pages, 61 illustrations, 4 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-926213-7 hardback £25 & $45. - Rainer Vollkommer. Neue Sternstunden der Archäologie. 2006. 278 pages, 36 illustrations. München; Beck; 978-3406-55058-4 paperback. - Matthew JOHNSON. Ideas of landscape. xxii+242 pages, 34 illustrations. 2006. Malden (MA), Oxford & Victoria (Aus); Blackwell; 978-1405-101592 hardback £50 & $74.95 & AUS$165; 978-14051016-08 paperback £19.99 & $34.95 & AUS$55.95 - Neil Brodie, Morag M. Kersel, Christina Luke & Kathryn Walker Tubb (ed.). Archaeology, Cultural Heritage, and the Antiquities Trade. xiv+350 pages, 33 illustrations, 3 tables. 2006. Gainesville (FL): University Press of Florida; 0-8130-2972-4 hardback $65. - Ian Russell (ed.). Images, Representations and Heritage: Moving beyond Modern Approaches to Archaeology. xxiv+390 pages, 54 illustrations, 4 tables. 2006. New York: Springer; 978-038732215-5 hardback $79.95. - John Chapman & Bisserka Gaydarska. Parts and Wholes: Fragmentation in Prehistoric Context. xiv+250 pages, 123 illustrations, 43 colour plates, 38 tables. 2007. Oxford: Oxbow; 978-1-84217-2223 paperback £30. - N. Steven Archer & Kevin M. Bartoy (ed.). Between Dirt and Discussion: Methods, Methodology, and Interpretation in Historical Archaeology. xiv+236 pages, 50 illustrations, 12 tables. 2006. New York: Springer; 978-0387-34218-4 hardback $99\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"General - Graeme Barker. The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: Why did foragers become farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-9281091 hardback £80. - Roy Ellen (ed.). Ethnobiology and the Science of Humankind (Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute Special Issue 1). viii+202 pages, 19 illu-strations, 8 tables. 2006. Oxford, Malden (MA) & Victoria, Australia: Blackwell; 978-1-4051-4589-7 paperback £19.99 & $34.95 & AUS$65.95. - Frederic Seller, Russell Greaves & Pei-Lin YU (ed.). Archaeology and Ethnoarchaeology ofMobility. xviii+290 pages, 54 illustrations, 28 tables. 2006. Gainesville (FL): University Press of Florida; 0-81302956-2 hardback $65. - Isabelle Sidera with Emmanuelle Vila & Phillippe Erikson (ed.). La chasse: pratiques sociales et symboliques (Colloques de la Maison René-Ginouvès). xiv+266 pages, 87 illustrations. 2006. Paris: De Boccard; 2-7018-0192-3 paperback. - Michael Bright. Beasts of the Field: The Revealing NaturalHistoryofAnimalsinthe Bible. xviii+346 pages. 2006. London: Robson Books; 978-1-86105-831-7 paperback £12.99 - Azar Gat. War in Human Civilization. xvi+822 pages, 61 illustrations, 4 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-926213-7 hardback £25 & $45. - Rainer Vollkommer. Neue Sternstunden der Archäologie. 2006. 278 pages, 36 illustrations. München; Beck; 978-3406-55058-4 paperback. - Matthew JOHNSON. Ideas of landscape. xxii+242 pages, 34 illustrations. 2006. Malden (MA), Oxford & Victoria (Aus); Blackwell; 978-1405-101592 hardback £50 & $74.95 & AUS$165; 978-14051016-08 paperback £19.99 & $34.95 & AUS$55.95 - Neil Brodie, Morag M. Kersel, Christina Luke & Kathryn Walker Tubb (ed.). Archaeology, Cultural Heritage, and the Antiquities Trade. xiv+350 pages, 33 illustrations, 3 tables. 2006. Gainesville (FL): University Press of Florida; 0-8130-2972-4 hardback $65. - Ian Russell (ed.). Images, Representations and Heritage: Moving beyond Modern Approaches to Archaeology. xxiv+390 pages, 54 illustrations, 4 tables. 2006. New York: Springer; 978-038732215-5 hardback $79.95. - John Chapman & Bisserka Gaydarska. Parts and Wholes: Fragmentation in Prehistoric Context. xiv+250 pages, 123 illustrations, 43 colour plates, 38 tables. 2007. Oxford: Oxbow; 978-1-84217-2223 paperback £30. - N. Steven Archer & Kevin M. Bartoy (ed.). Between Dirt and Discussion: Methods, Methodology, and Interpretation in Historical Archaeology. xiv+236 pages, 50 illustrations, 12 tables. 2006. New York: Springer; 978-0387-34218-4 hardback $99\"},\"creator\":\"Madeleine Hummler\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Madeleine\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Hummler\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2007\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[],\"publication\":\"Antiquity, 81, 20070301, 245\",\"summaries\":[],\"summary\":\"\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"5722248338\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Graeme Barker The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: why did Foragers become Farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £80\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Graeme Barker The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: why did Foragers become Farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £80\"},\"creator\":\"Peter Rowley-Conwy\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Peter\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Rowley-Conwy\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2007\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[],\"publication\":\"Antiquity, 81, 200712, 1099\",\"summaries\":[],\"summary\":\"\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"5132400674\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Errakina: Pastoral Fire Use and Landscape Memory In the Basque Region of the French Western Pyrenees\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Errakina: Pastoral Fire Use and Landscape Memory In the Basque Region of the French Western Pyrenees\"},\"creator\":\"Michael R. Coughlan\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Michael R.\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Coughlan\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2013\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"fire use\",\"fire management\",\"historical ecology\",\"landscape memory\",\"French Western Pyrenees\"],\"publication\":\"Journal of Ethnobiology, 33, 06 2013, 86\",\"summaries\":[\"People in the French Western Pyrenees have used fire for millennia in order to shape and manage landscapes. This history has left cultural and ecological legacies that both reflect and ensure the relative persistence of landscape patterns and processes. In this paper I draw on ethnographic research, ethnohistorical evidence, and Bayesian spatial analyses of historical fire use locations and land use maps to shed some light on human-fire-landscape dynamics in the Pyrenees for the years 1830 to 2011. I show how cultural and ecological legacies reflect a self-organized fire management regime that emerges from fire use driven by the production goals of individual households. I frame the self-organizing dynamic inherent in Pyrenean pastoral fire use as “landscape memory.” This conclusion has implications for the future direction of fire-related conservation policy for the Pyrenees and for analogous systems characterized by self-organized land management regimes\"],\"summary\":\"People in the French Western Pyrenees have used fire for millennia in order to shape and manage landscapes. This history has left cultural and ecological legacies that both reflect and ensure the relative persistence of landscape patterns and processes. In this paper I draw on ethnographic research, ethnohistorical evidence, and Bayesian spatial analyses of historical fire use locations and land use maps to shed some light on human-fire-landscape dynamics in the Pyrenees for the years 1830 to 2011. I show how cultural and ecological legacies reflect a self-organized fire management regime that emerges from fire use driven by the production goals of individual households. I frame the self-organizing dynamic inherent in Pyrenean pastoral fire use as “landscape memory.” This conclusion has implications for the future direction of fire-related conservation policy for the Pyrenees and for analogous systems characterized by self-organized land management regimes\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"8272251044\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"BarkerGraeme             The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: why did Foragers become Farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £80\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"BarkerGraeme             The Agricultural Revolution in Prehistory: why did Foragers become Farmers? xvi+598 pages, 138 illustrations, 15 tables. 2006. Oxford: Oxford University Press; 978-0-19-928109-1 hardback £80\"},\"creator\":\"Peter Rowley-Conwy\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Peter\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Rowley-Conwy\"}}],\"publicationDate\":null,\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[],\"publication\":\"Antiquity, 81, 20071201, 1099\",\"summaries\":[],\"summary\":\"\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"46732429\",\"isbns\":[\"0534581943\",\"9780534581947\"],\"isbn13\":\"9780534581947\",\"title\":\"Understanding physical anthropology and archaeology\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Understanding physical anthropology and archaeology\"},\"creator\":\"William A. Turnbaugh\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"William A.\"},\"isPrimary\":false,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Turnbaugh\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"©2002\",\"publicationLanguage\":\"eng\",\"catalogingLanguage\":\"eng\",\"generalFormat\":\"Book\",\"specificFormat\":\"PrintBook\",\"edition\":\"8th ed\",\"totalEditions\":5,\"publisher\":\"Wadsworth Thomson Learning\",\"publicationPlace\":\"Belmont, CA\",\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"Physical anthropology\",\"Archaeology\",\"Anthropologie physique\",\"Archéologie\",\"physical anthropology\",\"archaeology\"],\"publication\":null,\"summaries\":[],\"summary\":\"\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":[{\"generalFormat\":\"Book\",\"oclcNumber\":\"1036887060\",\"specificFormat\":\"Digital\"}],\"peerReviewed\":false,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"4630494199\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Informal Planting of Squashes and Gourds by Rural Farmers in Southwestern Tamaulipas, Mexico, and Implications for the Local Adoption of Food Production in Prehistory\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Informal Planting of Squashes and Gourds by Rural Farmers in Southwestern Tamaulipas, Mexico, and Implications for the Local Adoption of Food Production in Prehistory\"},\"creator\":\"J. Kevin Hanselka\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"J. Kevin\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Hanselka\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2010\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"Prehistoric Mexico\",\"Tamaulipas\",\"agricultural origins\",\"low-level food production\",\"squashes and gourds\"],\"publication\":\"Journal of Ethnobiology, 30, 03 2010, 31\",\"summaries\":[\"Present-day small-scale farmers near Ocampo, Tamaulipas, Mexico demonstrate behaviors that have broader implications for prehistoric human ecology. Common incidences of casual, informal cultivation of squashes and gourds take place around routine seasonal activities, often kilometers away from homes and conventional agricultural plots. These observations elucidate prehistoric subsistence behaviors in a region known for archaeological evidence of low-level food production. Such activities may serve as analogies for behaviors characterizing the extended period following the arrival of cultigens in Ocampo about 6,500&nbsp;years ago, until the establishment of the first settled agricultural villages around 3,500&nbsp;years ago. They demonstrate that domesticated squashes and gourds are sufficiently resilient to be left unattended for months at a time and still produce viable fruits to be collected and used when the cultivators return. Thus their use is compatible with a nomadic settlement system, and their adoption did not necessitate a drastic reduction in mobility. Contemporary cropping strategies illuminate how some plants that were fully domesticated by the time they arrived in Ocampo could have been successfully integrated into an otherwise hunter-gatherer lifestyle without major economic disruptions\"],\"summary\":\"Present-day small-scale farmers near Ocampo, Tamaulipas, Mexico demonstrate behaviors that have broader implications for prehistoric human ecology. Common incidences of casual, informal cultivation of squashes and gourds take place around routine seasonal activities, often kilometers away from homes and conventional agricultural plots. These observations elucidate prehistoric subsistence behaviors in a region known for archaeological evidence of low-level food production. Such activities may serve as analogies for behaviors characterizing the extended period following the arrival of cultigens in Ocampo about 6,500&nbsp;years ago, until the establishment of the first settled agricultural villages around 3,500&nbsp;years ago. They demonstrate that domesticated squashes and gourds are sufficiently resilient to be left unattended for months at a time and still produce viable fruits to be collected and used when the cultivators return. Thus their use is compatible with a nomadic settlement system, and their adoption did not necessitate a drastic reduction in mobility. Contemporary cropping strategies illuminate how some plants that were fully domesticated by the time they arrived in Ocampo could have been successfully integrated into an otherwise hunter-gatherer lifestyle without major economic disruptions\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"9815258413\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Implications of Religion Engagement and Development Projects on Gender Equality: A Case in Tanzania – Sub-Saharan Africa\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Implications of Religion Engagement and Development Projects on Gender Equality: A Case in Tanzania – Sub-Saharan Africa\"},\"creator\":\"Robert W. Kisusu\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Robert W.\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Kisusu\"}},{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Samson T.\"},\"isPrimary\":false,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Tongori\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2023\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"development projects\",\"gender equality\",\"gender gap index\",\"Implication\",\"policy\",\"religion engagement\",\"Tanzania,\"],\"publication\":\"International Journal of Political Activism and Engagement (IJPAE), 10, 20230317, 1\",\"summaries\":[\"The study rationalized how development projects implemented through religion engagement (RE) implicates satisfactorily attaining gender equality. Reorganized establishment based on secondary data analyzed using qualitative and quantitative techniques found how social development projects implemented through RE increased gender equality, thus reducing gender gap index. And this is evidenced by how the gender gap index in Tanzania declined from 0.652 to 0.537 between 1995 and 2017. However, it was high, as by 2022 the gender gap index recorded 0.72 in Tanzania. This attributed by several factors, including agricultural and livestock development projects, increased gender inequality as men dominate more than women, and causal factors are that women lack capital and are inaccessible to credit services as they lack mortgage assets. The conclusion shows development projects implemented through RE are significantly increasing gender equality, but a gap remains a crucial problem facing Tanzanians and recommendations geared to policy makers to employ effective policies and adopt practical modalities\"],\"summary\":\"The study rationalized how development projects implemented through religion engagement (RE) implicates satisfactorily attaining gender equality. Reorganized establishment based on secondary data analyzed using qualitative and quantitative techniques found how social development projects implemented through RE increased gender equality, thus reducing gender gap index. And this is evidenced by how the gender gap index in Tanzania declined from 0.652 to 0.537 between 1995 and 2017. However, it was high, as by 2022 the gender gap index recorded 0.72 in Tanzania. This attributed by several factors, including agricultural and livestock development projects, increased gender inequality as men dominate more than women, and causal factors are that women lack capital and are inaccessible to credit services as they lack mortgage assets. The conclusion shows development projects implemented through RE are significantly increasing gender equality, but a gap remains a crucial problem facing Tanzanians and recommendations geared to policy makers to employ effective policies and adopt practical modalities\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":false,\"openAccessLink\":null},{\"oclcNumber\":\"4630556195\",\"isbns\":[],\"isbn13\":\"\",\"title\":\"Climate and Demography in Early Prehistory: Using Calibrated 14 C Dates as Population Proxies\",\"titleInfo\":{\"text\":\"Climate and Demography in Early Prehistory: Using Calibrated 14 C Dates as Population Proxies\"},\"creator\":\"Felix Riede\",\"contributors\":[{\"firstName\":{\"text\":\"Felix\"},\"isPrimary\":true,\"secondName\":{\"text\":\"Riede\"}}],\"publicationDate\":\"2009\",\"publicationLanguage\":null,\"catalogingLanguage\":null,\"generalFormat\":\"ArtChap\",\"specificFormat\":\"Artcl\",\"edition\":null,\"totalEditions\":null,\"publisher\":null,\"digitalObjectInfo\":null,\"subjects\":[\"14 C DATING\",\"DEMOGRAPHY\",\"PREHISTORY\",\"CLIMATIC STRESS\",\"MESOLITHIC\",\"PALEOLITHIC\",\"IRELAND\",\"BROMME CULTURE\",\"SCANDINAVIA\"],\"publication\":\"Human Biology, 81, 04 2009, 309\",\"summaries\":[\"Although difficult to estimate for prehistoric hunter-gatherer populations, demographic variables&mdash;population size, density, and the connectedness of demes&mdash;are critical for a better understanding of the processes of material culture change, especially in deep prehistory. Demography is the middle-range link between climatic changes and both biological and cultural evolutionary trajectories of human populations. Much of human material culture functions as a buffer against climatic changes, and the study of prehistoric population dynamics, estimated through changing frequencies of calibrated radiocarbon dates, therefore affords insights into how effectively such buffers operated and when they failed. In reviewing a number of case studies (Mesolithic Ireland, the origin of the Bromme culture, and the earliest late glacial human recolonization of southern Scandinavia), I suggest that a greater awareness of demographic processes, and in particular of demographic declines, provides many fresh insights into what structured the archaeological record. I argue that we cannot sideline climatic and environmental factors or extreme geophysical events in our reconstructions of prehistoric culture change. The implications of accepting demographic variability as a departure point for evaluating the archaeological record are discussed\"],\"summary\":\"Although difficult to estimate for prehistoric hunter-gatherer populations, demographic variables&mdash;population size, density, and the connectedness of demes&mdash;are critical for a better understanding of the processes of material culture change, especially in deep prehistory. Demography is the middle-range link between climatic changes and both biological and cultural evolutionary trajectories of human populations. Much of human material culture functions as a buffer against climatic changes, and the study of prehistoric population dynamics, estimated through changing frequencies of calibrated radiocarbon dates, therefore affords insights into how effectively such buffers operated and when they failed. In reviewing a number of case studies (Mesolithic Ireland, the origin of the Bromme culture, and the earliest late glacial human recolonization of southern Scandinavia), I suggest that a greater awareness of demographic processes, and in particular of demographic declines, provides many fresh insights into what structured the archaeological record. I argue that we cannot sideline climatic and environmental factors or extreme geophysical events in our reconstructions of prehistoric culture change. The implications of accepting demographic variability as a departure point for evaluating the archaeological record are discussed\",\"abstract\":null,\"otherFormats\":null,\"peerReviewed\":true,\"openAccessLink\":null}]}"
          },
